WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.060
- I think one of the most
common problems that people have

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.660
is not that they don't know
what to do to dramatically

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.410
move their life forward
in a substantial way.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.200
You know exactly what
you need to do right now.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.480
There's probably something
that you've been avoiding

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.350
like the plague,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.050
but the problem is not that
you don't know what that is.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.440
The problem is that you just
can't bring yourself to do it.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.320
And that's the common
theme every single day is

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.690
that when you sit down to do this thing,

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.110
you feel a feeling and
it almost seems unfair

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.810
how potent this feeling of resistance is

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.300
because it's usually only
present when you think

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.390
about doing things that
are genuinely good for you.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.190
So it's like, what's the deal with that?

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.770
Why does it seem like
life is set up that way?

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.820
Wouldn't it be better

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.540
if you just actually enjoyed
doing difficult things?

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.470
Wouldn't it be better

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.010
if you actually had the
motivation to be creative

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.980
or to write a paper or to do your taxes?

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.230
You probably want nothing more

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.870
than to be someone who genuinely enjoys

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.560
and looks forward to doing the
things that are good for you,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.240
doing the things that empower you.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.920
Recently I've been thinking.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.650
There is one strategy that I have

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.110
that works every single time.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.500
It always makes me do the
thing that I need to do.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.020
It's not very flattering.
It's not very sexy.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.930
We don't want to believe
that this is the answer.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.070
And I feel like there's so much

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.290
self-improvement advice out there that

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.950
kind of ignores this
fundamental, not even tactic,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.690
it's almost a fact of life.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.220
And when we ignore this fact,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.490
we'll try to do little
tips and tricks like, oh,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.260
set a timer for five minutes

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.990
and just really try, try
really hard to do the work,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.750
or try to think more positive
thoughts about your work.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.460
But all those strategies,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.230
they're kind of throw away strategies.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.820
Yeah, they might work here or there,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.170
but they get old really quick.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.910
This strategy is nuclear.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.180
So if you need to do one particular thing,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.310
then if you do this, you will do it.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.360
You will have no choice.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.640
The solution is boredom.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.220
Ironically that's also the problem.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.950
The solution and the
problem are the same thing.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.540
You don't wanna do that work

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.970
because it's so boring to your brain,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:10.920
you want to gouge your eyes out.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.960
All of these distractions
that you have around

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.230
sound so much more entertaining
than doing that thing.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.030
So you always have a backdoor.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.430
Why in hell would you write an essay

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.170
when you could be checking Instagram?

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.780
When you could be watching
YouTube videos like this one?

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.490
But the key realization is

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.050
that your brain doesn't see
objective entertainment values.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.760
It's always relative.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.030
A good analogy for this is
when you're spending all day

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.820
outside in the sun,
it's a super bright day,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.610
and then you walk into
a dim room or a store,

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.800
that indoor space feels super dark.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.540
You're like, I can't see
anything. What is this?

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.250
And then you see that weird

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.670
fake sun in your periphery
that starts to fade.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.350
Everything looks weird and purplely.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.640
And in a very similar way,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.260
if you've been asleep for a couple hours

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.480
and you have to get up
and go to the bathroom,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.820
you turn the lights on
and it's just blinding.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.640
It's not that that
bathroom is any more bright

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.770
than it was before you went to bed

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.000
when you were brushing your teeth,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.750
it's just that your brain
interprets things using contrasts.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.070
It uses comparison.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.700
So the key is to utterly
delete the back doors.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.090
The key is to strip away
every single option,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:21.270
to sit in a blank room with nothing in it,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.190
no phone, nothing.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.910
Eliminate your ability
to do anything other

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.090
than the thing that you need
to do and you will do it.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.460
And now you might be thinking,
oh, it's not that simple.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.720
I can just do nothing.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.550
Well, you actually won't do nothing.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.930
And there's studies to prove it.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.893
There is a really weird study
published in Science in 2014.

00:03:40.893 --> 00:03:42.480
That was a mouthful, it's not.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.190
What am I, a freaking Neanderthal?

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.060
That's not a mouthful.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.470
That was really hard to say.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.580
So in this study,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.710
they got a bunch of undergraduate students

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.590
to sit in a empty room by themselves

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.414
with absolutely nothing to do

00:03:55.414 --> 00:03:56.490
and be alone with their own thoughts.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.750
But they had one other option.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.900
And that was to electrocute
themselves with a shocky button.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.900
And it turns out 67% of the
men and 25% of the women

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.480
decided that electrocution
was better than boredom.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.440
And some of the students

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.390
electrocuted themselves multiple times.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.120
Even the ones that didn't
electrocute themselves

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.360
either strongly considered
it or at least lamented as

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.950
to how miserable the quiet
time was and how boring it was.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.630
You might have heard
about this study before,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.070
but I think it's a great example

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.877
as to how much human
beings dislike being bored.

00:04:29.877 --> 00:04:33.210
And so the reason why solitary
confinement is considered one

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.540
of the least humane forms
of torture imaginable.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.640
There's this ancient
torture method where they

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.910
basically just throw somebody
down a hole under the castle

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.673
and forget about them
for their entire lives.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.877
Number 15, the oubliette.

00:04:47.877 --> 00:04:50.460
The last thing you'd
want after stealing a cup

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.517
of buckwheat is to end up in
one of these mother (bleep).

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.420
That sounds terrible
because it's so boring.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.970
If you were put in an empty
room with nothing to do

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.440
except one thing,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.780
unless that thing is
like chop off your hand,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.220
you're probably gonna do that thing.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.810
And while you're doing that thing,

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.780
that thing will feel so
much more entertaining

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:10.770
than doing nothing.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.210
So you have to ask yourself,

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.850
what are you constructing
your life around?

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.680
How are you structuring things?

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.920
Are the things that you're
supposed to do entertaining

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.830
in comparison to the things
that you use as distractions?

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.020
Because the larger the disparity,

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.720
the more agonizing that thing will be.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.600
And you'll find very quickly
that if you delete distractions

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.120
as an option, it works right away.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:33.953
It's insane.

00:05:33.953 --> 00:05:35.850
This isn't like some dopamine detox

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.430
that you gotta do over
weeks and months and years,

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.980
although that will help
you get used to finding

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.020
the entertainment value
in difficult things.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.090
This is something that works right away

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.816
because your brain needs to to survive.

00:05:48.816 --> 00:05:49.860
It hates feeling bored.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.140
So it will make whatever
your only option is

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.090
as entertaining as it needs to.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.190
I even have personal evidence for this.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.680
One of the most productive, social,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.170
and energetic times in my entire life

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.320
is when I was living in
a little 900 square foot

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.990
basement suite in the middle of suburbia

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.100
